<!--
  Undock: switch the gait to MPC, back straight out
  1 m from where the robot stands with the controller's DockToPose
  (controller_id "Undocking"), then return to RL locomotion. Selected per
  goal by NavigateToGoal.behavior_tree ("undock.xml" or the absolute share
  path).

  The goal's `pose` is IGNORED (2026-10-15): send it empty. The back-out
  target is ComputeBackOutPose's (-1, 0) in the robot's own base frame,
  which controller_server transforms into odom once, when the back-out
  starts -- "1 m behind where the robot stands now", same heading. Until
  then the goal carried the dock pose and ComputeStagingPose derived the
  target from it, which made the backend track which dock the robot was
  lying on to get it off again. The robot's own pose is that dock pose to
  within the dock goal checker's 5 cm / 0.05 rad, so nothing was gained.
  One consequence: a re-sent undock backs out another metre from wherever
  the robot is, not to a fixed staging pose.

  Starting state: the robot is STANDING on the dock in MPC, which is where
  dock.xml ends since 2026-10-15. The first step is key="5" (MPC) for the
  slow, precise walk, exactly as dock.xml; for a robot already in MPC it
  only re-confirms the mode.

  No stand-up step (removed 2026-10-15). From 2026-10-14 the tree began
  with SetMotionKey key="0" (`MODE Z`, Stand) and a guessed 4000 ms Wait,
  because dock.xml then ended with the robot lying down. Once dock.xml
  stopped lying it down, that key was assumed to be a no-op for a standing
  robot, and it is not: on robot01 the robot was seen going down right
  after a dock that sent no lie-down, with this key the only command in
  between (the backend sent the undock 0.3 s after the dock succeeded), and
  getting up again for the back-out. A robot LYING on the dock -- a task
  that sent LIEDOWN after the dock -- is therefore not handled here: what
  MPC does from lying is unmeasured, so stand it up first (the backend's
  STANDUP step) before sending this tree.

  Why not a plain MOVE off the dock: the robot's footprint sits on the
  dock's lethal cells in the local costmap, so MPPI (the FollowPath
  controller) starts in collision and cannot be relied on to get off it; a
  mode switch and a navigation would also be two steps the backend has to
  sequence. The back-out is blind, like the approach -- the docking
  controller reads no costmap -- which is the point.

  Why backward rather than turning on the dock: the charging contacts are
  under the robot, an in-place turn on them is wear nobody has measured, and
  the space beside the dock is unknown; backing out along the line it came
  in on is the one motion known to be clear (opennav_docking undocks the
  same way). The Undocking controller instance reverses by itself when the
  target is behind it (allow_backward, no initial rotation), and the target
  keeps the robot's heading, so it ends facing the dock 1 m out and the MOVE
  that follows starts from a known heading.

  The MPC switch is CHECKED, as in dock.xml (2026-10-14): SetMotionKey is a
  one-way UDP datagram, so WaitForMotionState waits (up to 5 s) for the
  controller to report MPC -- 5 on the `mode` topic's data[1], read off the
  robot -- and a Wait of 3000 ms then lets the gait settle before the robot
  moves; see dock.xml's header. Raised 2000 -> 3000 ms on 2026-10-15
  together with dock.xml's: the settling is the same gait transition in
  both trees, so the two numbers move together.

  Gait on exit: RL (key="1") on success, since a MOVE follows -- unlike
  dock.xml, which ends on the dock in MPC and never switches back. The
  switch back is CHECKED like the one into MPC (2026-10-14):
  WaitForMotionState waits (up to 5 s) for Locomotion, 1 on `mode` data[1],
  so SUCCEEDED means the robot is walking in RL and the MOVE that follows
  will not be sent to a gait still in transition. No settling Wait after it:
  the next step is a separate goal, which already costs a round trip through
  the backend. If RL is never reported, the step fails inside the Sequence
  and the Fallback sends key="1" once more (RestoreRL) before the goal ends
  DOCK_FAILED -- one retry of the same key, harmless. On any failure after
  the MPC switch was attempted -- the MPC state never reported included --
  the Fallback's second child restores RL as well. A CANCEL during the back-out
  halts the tree without the restore, leaving MPC on with the robot standing
  somewhere in the metre behind the dock; re-sending this goal backs out a
  further metre from there (see above), or the backend restores RL itself.

  Attribution and status sentences follow dock.xml: SetMotionKey and
  DockToPose write themselves on failure and the navigator maps them to
  DOCK_FAILED (the DockToPose message says back-out, not approach);
  SetBlackboard writes the tree's own sentences ("Switching the gait to MPC
  for undocking", "Switching the gait back to RL"), DockToPose writes "Backing out of the
  dock", "Undocking: 0.42 m to the staging pose", "Undocked".
  ComputeBackOutPose fails only on a misconfigured tree or node (distance,
  base_frame) and is mapped to DOCK_FAILED like the rest. There is no
  path, so the navigator's distance_remaining reads 0 throughout; progress is
  in status_msg.
-->
<root main_tree_to_execute="MainTree">
  <BehaviorTree ID="MainTree">
    <Sequence name="Undock">
      <!-- No stand-up step: the robot is already standing in MPC (header) -->
      <Fallback name="UndockInMpc">
        <Sequence name="SwitchAndBackOut">
          <SetBlackboard output_key="nav_status_msg" value="Switching the gait to MPC for undocking"/>
          <SetMotionKey key="5" server_timeout="500"/>
          <!-- Confirm the controller reports MPC (mode data[1] == 5), then let it settle (header) -->
          <WaitForMotionState state="5" timeout="5000"/>
          <Wait msec="3000"/>
          <!-- 1 m behind the robot, in its base frame; the goal's pose is not read (header) -->
          <ComputeBackOutPose distance="1.0" back_out_pose="{back_out_pose}"/>
          <DockToPose dock_pose="{back_out_pose}" controller_id="Undocking" goal_checker_id="dock_goal_checker" timeout="60.0"/>
          <SetBlackboard output_key="nav_status_msg" value="Switching the gait back to RL"/>
          <SetMotionKey name="BackToRL" key="1" server_timeout="500"/>
          <!-- Confirm the controller reports RL locomotion (mode data[1] == 1) before SUCCEEDED (header) -->
          <WaitForMotionState state="1" timeout="5000"/>
        </Sequence>
        <ForceFailure>
          <SetMotionKey name="RestoreRL" key="1" server_timeout="500"/>
        </ForceFailure>
      </Fallback>
    </Sequence>
  </BehaviorTree>
</root>
